LXNU Component Boundaries and Interface Contracts

Project: LXNU / System-One
Architecture: AMD64
Version: 1.6.6.2ɓ
Status: Engineering specification
Execution status: NOT RUN

1. Purpose

This document defines the ownership boundaries, responsibilities, dependencies, and interface contracts of the LXNU and System-One architecture.

The objective is to prevent duplicated functionality, circular dependencies, uncontrolled coupling, and unclear responsibility between components.

Each component must have a defined purpose, explicit interfaces, and independently reviewable implementation requirements.

2. Architectural Principles

The architecture follows these principles:

- Each component has a clearly defined responsibility.
- Interfaces must be explicit and documented.
- Dependencies must flow in controlled directions.
- Circular dependencies are prohibited unless specifically justified and approved.
- Shared functionality must have one authoritative owner.
- Components must not silently duplicate another component's responsibilities.
- Hardware-specific functionality must use validated interfaces.
- Performance and compatibility claims require measurable evidence.
- Component boundaries must preserve upstream Linux compatibility.

3. Architectural Layers

System-One is organized into the following conceptual layers:

Layer| Responsibility
Hardware| AMD CPUs, Radeon GPUs, XDNA NPUs and peripherals
Kernel| Linux kernel and LXNU integration
Platform| Device discovery, security, storage and system services
Performance| OmniVec and Omni Performance Libraries
Workload| OmniRed workload coordination
Application| OmniSoft and OmniApp
Graphics| Photon desktop and graphics integration
System integration| System-One orchestration and release integration

These layers define architectural ownership, not a claim that all components have been implemented.

4. LXNU Kernel Boundary

LXNU owns the kernel engineering and integration policy.

Upstream Linux remains authoritative for:

- Process scheduling.
- Virtual memory.
- Filesystem infrastructure.
- Networking.
- Device-driver infrastructure.
- Interrupt handling.
- Kernel synchronization.
- Hardware interfaces.
- Core security mechanisms.

LXNU may introduce documented architectural adaptations and integration changes inspired by selected XNU design patterns.

LXNU must not claim Mach source, Mach kernel compatibility, or a complete XNU implementation.

Userspace services must not independently replace kernel responsibilities.

5. OmniSoft Boundary

OmniSoft owns the userspace platform and application-support environment.

Its responsibilities include:

- Runtime infrastructure.
- Application integration.
- Platform libraries.
- System-facing userspace interfaces.
- Application lifecycle support.
- Approved application packaging interfaces.

OmniSoft must consume documented LXNU and System-One interfaces.

It must not duplicate kernel scheduling, memory management, or device-driver ownership.

6. OmniRed Boundary

OmniRed owns workload coordination and workload-policy integration.

Its responsibilities include:

- Workload classification.
- Execution profiles.
- Resource coordination.
- Workload scheduling requests through approved interfaces.
- Performance policy integration.
- Workload monitoring and validation.

OmniRed does not replace the Linux scheduler or independently own hardware drivers.

Its profiles include:

- Interactive.
- Throughput.
- Real-time.
- Validated.
- Efficiency.

A workload policy does not guarantee a performance result.

7. Omni Performance Libraries Boundary

Omni Performance Libraries provide optimized computational and multimedia functionality.

Their responsibilities include:

- Approved optimized routines.
- Runtime capability detection.
- CPU and GPU dispatch.
- Validated fallback implementations.
- Performance benchmarking.
- Numerical correctness testing.

They must not assume optional CPU instructions are available.

The general userspace baseline remains x86-64-v3. Optional x86-64-v4 implementations require runtime feature detection and safe fallback paths.

Library availability does not establish acceleration or performance improvement.

8. OmniVec Boundary

OmniVec owns vector-oriented computational architecture and related dispatch interfaces.

It may coordinate suitable computational backends, including CPU and GPU implementations, through documented interfaces.

OmniVec must not become an alternative owner of:

- Kernel scheduling.
- Kernel memory management.
- Hardware driver initialization.
- Device firmware management.

Its implementation must distinguish architectural concepts, implemented functionality, and experimentally validated capabilities.

9. OmniSilicon Boundary

OmniSilicon owns AMD hardware identification, capability qualification, and hardware-specific integration records.

Its responsibilities include:

- CPU identification.
- GPU identification.
- NPU identification.
- Hardware capability records.
- Runtime feature verification.
- Qualification metadata.
- Hardware compatibility evidence.

OmniSilicon must consume kernel and driver interfaces rather than bypassing them.

Hardware detection must not be confused with hardware qualification.

10. Photon Boundary

Photon owns the System-One desktop and graphical user experience.

Its responsibilities include:

- Desktop shell.
- Window and workspace interaction.
- Application presentation.
- System indicators.
- Display integration.
- HDR user-interface integration.
- Desktop configuration and settings.

The intended desktop architecture uses Wayfire/wlroots with a Qt6 shell.

HDR requires an appropriately validated compositor, graphics-driver, display, and color-management pipeline.

Photon must not independently implement GPU kernel drivers or duplicate the underlying display-management infrastructure.

11. QCX Boundary

QCX (Quartz-Core X) owns its defined graphics and presentation integration responsibilities.

Its interfaces must remain distinct from Photon shell ownership and kernel graphics-driver ownership.

The relationship between QCX, OmniColor96-HDR, Photon, and underlying graphics interfaces must be documented explicitly.

QCX must not claim functionality based solely on an architectural mapping to another operating system's graphics framework.

12. OmniStatic Boundary

OmniStatic owns its defined static analysis, static artifact, and associated engineering integration responsibilities.

Its exact implementation scope must be established through component-level specifications.

It must not silently assume ownership of compiler infrastructure, package management, or kernel build orchestration.

Interfaces with LLVM, build tooling, and validation systems must be documented.

13. OCE Boundary

OCE owns the System-One compression-engine functionality defined by its specification.

OCE must remain one compression engine.

It must not be represented as multiple independent engines through artificial engine-count multipliers.

Its responsibilities include:

- Compression and decompression interfaces.
- Algorithm selection.
- File-layer integration where approved.
- Integrity and compatibility validation.
- Performance measurement.
- Documented fallback behavior.

The root image must remain compatible with the required zstd decoding path.

OCE must not make the root boot process dependent on an incompatible custom compression format.

14. OrbionFS Boundary

OrbionFS owns unified storage orchestration and filesystem-role policy.

It coordinates supported storage technologies, including:

- EROFS.
- OverlayFS.
- tmpfs.
- Btrfs.
- F2FS.
- XFS.

The System-One root architecture remains:

- EROFS read-only lower layer.
- dm-verity integrity verification.
- OverlayFS writable integration.
- tmpfs upper layer.

Persistent filesystem roles must follow explicit System-One storage policy.

tmpfs is part of OrbionFS orchestration, not a separate replacement storage architecture.

OrbionFS must not duplicate filesystem implementations or bypass the Linux VFS.

15. vCIM Boundary

vCIM owns its defined virtualized computational and integration interfaces.

Its implementation must document:

- Resource ownership.
- Memory boundaries.
- Workload interfaces.
- Synchronization requirements.
- Failure behavior.
- Security assumptions.

It must not independently claim ownership of kernel virtualization mechanisms or hardware acceleration without validated integration.

16. OmniApp Boundary

OmniApp owns application packaging and application integration interfaces.

Supported package concepts include:

- ".OAI"
- ".OAPP"

The format specification must define metadata, integrity, dependency handling, execution permissions, lifecycle, and compatibility.

Application packaging must remain distinct from the underlying system package-management engine.

17. System Integration Boundary

System-One owns integration of the independently defined components into a coherent platform.

Its responsibilities include:

- Component compatibility.
- Version coordination.
- Dependency governance.
- Build and release integration.
- Security policy coordination.
- Hardware qualification.
- Validation and acceptance gates.
- System-level recovery and update policy.

System-One must not become a duplicate implementation of every subsystem.

Each subsystem remains responsible for its implementation and documented interfaces.

18. Interface Contract Requirements

Every production component must document:

1. Component identity and version.
2. Functional ownership.
3. Public interfaces.
4. Internal interfaces where relevant.
5. Dependencies.
6. Data formats and structures.
7. ABI and compatibility requirements.
8. Error handling.
9. Security boundaries.
10. Resource ownership.
11. Testing requirements.
12. Versioning and deprecation policy.

Interfaces must have clear compatibility expectations.

Breaking interface changes require documented migration and validation plans.

19. Dependency Direction

The architecture should favor controlled dependency flow:

- Hardware interfaces are exposed through Linux and validated drivers.
- LXNU integrates and maintains kernel-level behavior.
- Platform services consume kernel interfaces.
- Performance libraries expose optimized computational interfaces.
- OmniRed coordinates workloads through approved platform interfaces.
- OmniSoft exposes application-facing platform functionality.
- Photon consumes desktop and graphics interfaces.
- System-One coordinates integration and release acceptance.

No component may assume unrestricted access to another component's private implementation.

20. Failure Isolation

Components must define how failures affect dependent services.

Requirements include:

- Explicit error propagation.
- Documented recovery behavior.
- Resource cleanup.
- Defined service dependencies.
- Avoidance of uncontrolled cascading failures.
- Security-preserving failure behavior.
- Clear ownership of recovery operations.

A component must not silently convert a critical failure into a successful status.

21. Component Qualification

Each component requires separate validation.

Qualification must include applicable:

- Interface tests.
- Dependency checks.
- Security review.
- Functional testing.
- Performance measurement.
- Failure and recovery testing.
- Compatibility testing.
- Reproducibility evidence.

Passing an individual component test does not automatically qualify the complete System-One platform.

22. Current Architecture Status

This document establishes intended ownership boundaries and interface requirements.

It does not establish that every named component exists as executable software.

